    "explanation": "To the rider: part of your fare is returned, SGD 13.70. To the driver: the deviation is established on this trip's own trace, no clause excuses it, and the 1.6 surge is left untouched.\n\n1. Deviation (POL-RD-01). The governing distance is the trace, not the rounded copy on the trip record: gps_trace.trace_distance_km is 23.97 km against the booked optimal of 18.0 km, an excess of 5.97 km (33.17%). Both gates — more than 20% and more than 0.8 km — are cleared, so the deviation is ESTABLISHED. It is not MATERIAL: 5.97 km is below both the 8.0 km absolute test and the 50% test, so POL-RD-06's profile consultation and its uplift are not engaged. In any event no pattern exists: deviation_flags_90d is 1 against 412 completed trips (0.24%). The lifetime prior_deviation_flags counter is context only under POL-GEN-01 and cannot be substituted.\n\n2. No excuse applies. POL-RD-03 needs a traffic_advisory; EV-ADVIS-00114-01 is MISSING (weight 0.0, issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT), so nothing exonerates. POL-RD-04 fails: the chat log shows the rider challenging the route ('adam road?? jurong is the other way') and the driver answering that he chose the corridor ('AYE jam boss, this way faster'), with no rider instruction and waypoint_hint null. POL-RD-05 fails: no endpoint was amended.\n\n3. The driver's request for escalation under POL-RD-10(5) is refused. His own account — 'AYE was crawling' / 'AYE jam' — is a general assertion that traffic was bad and names no artifact. POL-RD-08 provides that such an assertion, unsupported by a traffic_advisory ID, does not discharge the driver's burden; POL-RD-10(5) requires an exculpatory clause invoked against a cited artifact that is missing, and no such artifact was cited. The advisory's absence is noted but does not make the record undecidable — it leaves the driver's excuse unsupported, and the burden stays with him. No other escalation trigger fires: the trace is unimpaired for policy purposes (241 fixes, 10 s interval, 100% coverage, no gaps, max_gap_sec 0.0), all three artifacts carry trip_id TRP-00114, and every charge line has a counterpart in the trace.\n\n4. Fare (POL-RD-02). The trip is METERED. Using the surge_multiplier of 1.6 read from the fare breakdown, and crediting only the Kim Keat Link gantry (which lies on the optimal route): recomputed_fare = ((3.80 + 0.75 x 18.0 + 0.28 x 30) x 1.6) + 0.80 + 2.50 = 44.42. charged_total is 58.10. Refund = 58.10 - 44.42 = 13.68, rounded UP to SGD 13.70. That is above the SGD 0.50 de minimis floor and well below 90% of charged_total, so the outcome is PARTIAL_REFUND. The SGD 2.00 Adam Road gantry is off the optimal route and is ALREADY comprehended in this figure under POL-RD-09(1); it is itemised for transparency and is not added on top. No POL-RD-09(4) billing-integrity correction arises: the trace passes 0.0 m from the Kim Keat Link gantry and 8.76 m from the Adam Road gantry, so both are matched crossings. No driver compensation is due (billed distance 24.0 km exceeds the trace, so there is no unbilled shortfall). No referral is produced by any clause. The 1.6 surge on base, distance and time stands and is not refunded."
